is insisting on leaving,

247
00:09:35,408 --> 00:09:37,284
and that MVA is here.

248
00:09:37,285 --> 00:09:39,036
- Okay, I'll be right there.

249
00:09:39,037 --> 00:09:41,038
- What's open?
- Uh, South 15.

250
00:09:41,039 --> 00:09:42,372
- 78-year-old woman,

251
00:09:42,373 --> 00:09:44,041
very low speed
auto versus pedestrian

252
00:09:44,042 --> 00:09:45,584
with ground-level fall.

253
00:09:45,585 --> 00:09:47,127
Bruising on her left hip.
No head trauma.

254
00:09:47,128 --> 00:09:48,754
- Her husband,
Eddie, came along.

255
00:09:48,755 --> 00:09:50,631
- Hi. How do you do, sir?
I'm Dr. Robby. Come with us.

256
00:09:50,632 --> 00:09:53,092
We're gonna get you
set up right over here.

257
00:09:53,093 --> 00:09:54,843
- [shouts in pain]
- Sorry.

258
00:09:54,844 --> 00:09:56,595
We'll get you something
for the pain shortly.

259
00:09:56,596 --> 00:09:58,055
- Thank you.

260
00:09:58,056 --> 00:10:00,057
- Do you have
any medical problems, Frida?

261
00:10:00,058 --> 00:10:04,228
- Uh, high blood pressure,
low thyroid, um, AFib.

262
00:10:04,229 --> 00:10:06,438
- Any chest pain,
shortness of breath?

263
00:10:06,439 --> 00:10:08,107
- No.
- Front-row seat, sir.

264
00:10:08,108 --> 00:10:10,067
- So, um, what happened?

265
00:10:10,068 --> 00:10:13,445
- Well, she walked
into the car.

266
00:10:13,446 --> 00:10:15,531
- [scoffs]
You hit me.

267
00:10:15,532 --> 00:10:18,534
- No, I didn't hit you.
You hit the car.

268
00:10:18,535 --> 00:10:20,577
- You backed up right into me.

269
00:10:20,578 --> 00:10:23,247
- I wasn't even moving.
- [chuckles]

270
00:10:23,248 --> 00:10:25,582
Well, you're both
in very good hands now.

271
00:10:25,583 --> 00:10:27,376
Yes?

272
00:10:27,377 --> 00:10:30,921
- Before we get you in a gown,
we'd like to check your hip.

273
00:10:30,922 --> 00:10:32,632
- Of course.

274
00:10:34,134 --> 00:10:35,592
- I am sorry.

275
00:10:35,593 --> 00:10:37,344
We had a whole bunch
of craziness come in,

276
00:10:37,345 --> 00:10:40,264
and you got bumped down
a patient or two for CT.

277
00:10:40,265 --> 00:10:41,849
But take comfort in knowing

278
00:10:41,850 --> 00:10:43,600
it was
for some pretty nasty stuff.

279
00:10:43,601 --> 00:10:46,103
- How am I supposed
to find comfort in that

280
00:10:46,104 --> 00:10:47,980
or anything you've told me
since I got here,

281
00:10:47,981 --> 00:10:49,773
for that matter?
- I don't know.

282
00:10:49,774 --> 00:10:51,775
But everything is supposed
to come with a lesson, right?

283
00:10:51,776 --> 00:10:55,112
- Oh, this is more of your Zen
motorcycle bullshit.

284
00:10:55,113 --> 00:10:56,446
- Maybe.

285
00:10:56,447 --> 00:10:57,948
No, no, the truth is,
I'm just stalling

286
00:10:57,949 --> 00:10:59,616
'cause it's gonna be
a while longer.

287
00:10:59,617 --> 00:11:01,702
- Oh, hey, look,
I appreciate the effort,

288
00:11:01,703 --> 00:11:03,453
but you guys
are obviously jammed up.

289
00:11:03,454 --> 00:11:05,539
I'll just come back when it's
not our nation's birthday.

290
00:11:05,540 --> 00:11:06,999
- You're already here.

291
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:08,417
It's not gonna be
that much longer, I promise.

292
00:11:08,418 --> 00:11:09,626
Just stay and get your CT.

293
00:11:09,627 --> 00:11:11,962
- I will do it all
when you get back

294
00:11:11,963 --> 00:11:14,089
and this place is less crazy.
- This place is always crazy.

295
00:11:14,090 --> 00:11:15,924
I don't know
when I'm gonna get back.

296
00:11:15,925 --> 00:11:18,302
Can you just sit the fuck down
and wait a little bit longer?

297
00:11:18,303 --> 00:11:21,472
It won't kill you,
but leaving might.

298
00:11:23,141 --> 00:11:25,517
- [sighs]

299
00:11:25,518 --> 00:11:27,769
Okay.

300
00:11:27,770 --> 00:11:29,313
No problem.

301
00:11:29,314 --> 00:11:30,815
- Thank you.

302
00:11:32,317 --> 00:11:34,443
- That's a nasty bruise.

303
00:11:34,444 --> 00:11:38,155
- Do you take aspirin
or any blood thinners?

304
00:11:38,156 --> 00:11:41,325
- Uh, Eliquis for my AFib.

305
00:11:41,326 --> 00:11:44,077
- Any tenderness here?

306
00:11:44,078 --> 00:11:45,537
- No, not really.

307
00:11:45,538 --> 00:11:48,123
- Here?
- Oh, yeah, that hurts.

308
00:11:48,124 --> 00:11:50,375
- All right, 4 of, uh, morphine
and Zofran.

309
00:11:50,376 --> 00:11:54,004
- What about up here?
- Not really.

310
00:11:54,005 --> 00:11:56,548
- All right, so let's start
with labs and plain film.

311
00:11:56,549 --> 00:11:58,550
- No, CT abdomen pelvis.

312
00:11:58,551 --> 00:12:01,345
We'll get a better view of
the hip and check for bleeding.

313
00:12:01,346 --> 00:12:02,930
The Eliquis is concerning.

314
00:12:02,931 --> 00:12:05,891
- Uh, you think
she broke her hip?

315
00:12:05,892 --> 00:12:08,393
- If it's broken, you broke it.

316
00:12:08,394 --> 00:12:10,020
- Usually for a broken hip,

317
00:12:10,021 --> 00:12:12,564
the leg is shortened
and rotates out,

318
00:12:12,565 --> 00:12:14,859
but we'll just wait
for CT results.

319
00:12:21,824 --> 00:12:23,825
- Mr. Cohen...

320
00:12:23,826 --> 00:12:26,203
I know that you were checked
on scene,

321
00:12:26,204 --> 00:12:27,996
but I was wondering
if I could do

322
00:12:27,997 --> 00:12:29,581
a quick examination of you.

323
00:12:29,582 --> 00:12:31,708
- Oh, no, I'm fine.
I was in the car.

324
00:12:31,709 --> 00:12:33,461
- Just unofficially.

325
00:12:35,213 --> 00:12:37,339
- Okay.

326
00:12:37,340 --> 00:12:40,801
- Deep breath for me.
- [breathes deeply]

327
00:12:40,802 --> 00:12:42,552
- Good. Again.

328
00:12:42,553 --> 00:12:44,388
- [breathes deeply]

329
00:12:44,389 --> 00:12:46,390
- Great.
Thanks, Eddie.

330
00:12:46,391 --> 00:12:49,810
Next thing--would you mind
taking your shoes off?

331
00:12:49,811 --> 00:12:51,478
- Uh...

332
00:12:51,479 --> 00:12:52,813
what for?

333
00:12:52,814 --> 00:12:55,399
- Just being safe.

334
00:12:55,400 --> 00:12:57,234
- [groans]

335
00:12:57,235 --> 00:12:59,194
My back's not
what it used to be.

336
00:12:59,195 --> 00:13:00,446
- Let me help you.

337
00:13:04,826 --> 00:13:07,744
- I, uh, could use a pedicure.

338
00:13:07,745 --> 00:13:09,579
- You and me both.

339
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:13,458
Dr. King, what do you think
about doing a Romberg test?

340
00:13:13,459 --> 00:13:15,043
- What is that?

341
00:13:15,044 --> 00:13:17,421
- It's a general evaluation
of balance.

342
00:13:17,422 --> 00:13:20,090
Um, can you stand up, sir?

343
00:13:20,091 --> 00:13:21,967
- Yep.
[clears throat]

344
00:13:21,968 --> 00:13:23,927
[monitor beeping rapidly]
- How are you doing, Oliver?

345
00:13:23,928 --> 00:13:25,429
- Sats are not coming up.

346
00:13:25,430 --> 00:13:27,264
- Pressure's holding
on 400 of nitro.

347
00:13:27,265 --> 00:13:29,766
Let's up the BiPAP to 20 on 10.
- We could try phlebotomy.

348
00:13:29,767 --> 00:13:31,601
Decrease the fluid burden
on the lungs.

349
00:13:31,602 --> 00:13:33,770
- Old school,
but might buy time.

350
00:13:33,771 --> 00:13:35,105
- Hemoglobin is only 9.2.

351
00:13:35,106 --> 00:13:36,606
- He's anemic
from renal failure.

352
00:13:36,607 --> 00:13:38,233
- Then he can't afford
more blood loss.

353
00:13:38,234 --> 00:13:39,985
- We need to intubate.
We're losing this war.

354
00:13:39,986 --> 00:13:41,945
- Intubation's not
a trivial matter

355
00:13:41,946 --> 00:13:43,488
for immunosuppressed patients.

356
00:13:43,489 --> 00:13:45,365
- So we just drop
a bunch of red tops?

357
00:13:45,366 --> 00:13:46,992
- No, we do the collection
on the floor

358
00:13:46,993 --> 00:13:48,994
as if he's giving blood.
- Like medieval bloodletting.

359
00:13:48,995 --> 00:13:50,829
- Essentially,
and that is something

360
00:13:50,830 --> 00:13:52,289
you should step out for, Mason.

361
00:13:52,290 --> 00:13:54,833
Casey, thank you.
- Got a good vein for a 16.

362
00:13:54,834 --> 00:13:56,668
- One donor bag, 500 cc's.

363
00:13:56,669 --> 00:13:58,378
No more.

364
00:13:58,379 --> 00:14:00,672
- [inhales deeply]

365
00:14:00,673 --> 00:14:03,216
- Okay, let it out.
- [exhales]

366
00:14:03,217 --> 00:14:05,218
- Same again.
Deep breath in.

367
00:14:05,219 --> 00:14:07,512
- [breathes deeply]

368
00:14:07,513 --> 00:14:09,139
- Great.
All done.

369
00:14:09,140 --> 00:14:12,309
- How's she doing?
- Pulse ox 100%, normal exam.

370
00:14:12,310 --> 00:14:14,102
No evidence of injury.
- Great.

371
00:14:14,103 --> 00:14:15,812
- I got way worse
from my older brothers.

372
00:14:15,813 --> 00:14:17,272
- I bet.

373
00:14:17,273 --> 00:14:20,150
- All right, I'll go, uh,
write up her chart.

374
00:14:20,151 --> 00:14:22,486
- How about we call it a day?

375
00:14:22,487 --> 00:14:24,321
- My shift isn't over.

376
00:14:24,322 --> 00:14:27,866
- This was a shift and a half
for anyone today, believe me.

377
00:14:27,867 --> 00:14:31,161
- If it's all right with you,
I'd rather stay.

378
00:14:31,162 --> 00:14:32,662
I'm not a quitter.

379
00:14:32,663 --> 00:14:34,623
- Okay, Ms. Emma, good for you.

380
00:14:34,624 --> 00:14:37,542
But stick close to Donnie for
the rest of the day, would you?

381
00:14:37,543 --> 00:14:39,294
- Sure.

382
00:14:39,295 --> 00:14:42,297
- And you also need to make
a statement to the police.

383
00:14:42,298 --> 00:14:44,716
If we don't stand up for
ourselves, no one else will.

384
00:14:44,717 --> 00:14:48,345
We're here to help,
not to be punching bags.

385
00:14:48,346 --> 00:14:50,097
- Thank you for saving me.

386
00:14:50,098 --> 00:14:52,183
- I got you, girl.

387
00:14:54,394 --> 00:14:57,438
[indistinct chatter]

388
00:14:59,941 --> 00:15:02,192
- Something wrong?
- That depends.

389
00:15:02,193 --> 00:15:04,528
Are you a four-month-old
with severe diaper rash?

390
00:15:04,529 --> 00:15:06,113
- No, I'm Dorion Cole,

391
00:15:06,114 --> 00:15:07,906
and I'm pretty sure
that I broke my collarbone.

392
00:15:07,907 --> 00:15:10,200
- Okay, we can help with that.
I'll be back.

393
00:15:10,201 --> 00:15:11,785
- Wait, wait.
Are you serious?

394
00:15:11,786 --> 00:15:13,537
Can I at least get something
for my pain?

395
00:15:13,538 --> 00:15:15,622
- Sure. Sit tight.
- [scoffs] This place sucks.

396
00:15:15,623 --> 00:15:17,541
- Tell me about it.
Try working here.

397
00:15:17,542 --> 00:15:19,209
- Boy, you're
really working hard

398
00:15:19,210 --> 00:15:20,919
to boost those
patient satisfaction scores.

399
00:15:20,920 --> 00:15:22,379
- The chart for Central 7

400
00:15:22,380 --> 00:15:23,713
doesn't match
the patient that's in there.

401
00:15:23,714 --> 00:15:25,340
- Hang in there.
We're almost over this.

402
00:15:25,341 --> 00:15:26,925
- I was over this
six hours ago.

403
00:15:26,926 --> 00:15:29,386
- Chairs is looking like
Mullaney's on St. Paddy's Day.

404
00:15:29,387 --> 00:15:31,179
They're gonna break through
the doors like zombies...

405
00:15:31,180 --> 00:15:32,264
- Incoming.
- If we don't start moving

406
00:15:32,265 --> 00:15:33,390
more of the meat back there.

407
00:15:33,391 --> 00:15:35,183
- Dante Casella, 34.

408
00:15:35,184 --> 00:15:38,186
Blunt trauma from a fireworks
explosion in a storage unit.

409
00:15:38,187 --> 00:15:39,771
A and O, good vitals,

410
00:15:39,772 --> 00:15:42,065
large scalp laceration
and bruising to the chest.

411
00:15:42,066 --> 00:15:43,400
No meds or allergies.

412
00:15:43,401 --> 00:15:44,734
- Partial
or full thickness burns?

413
00:15:44,735 --> 00:15:46,403
- No burns.
The blast launched him

414
00:15:46,404 --> 00:15:48,113
into a rolled-down
garage door frame.

415
00:15:48,114 --> 00:15:50,449
- Am I fucking dying here?
- I'm Dr. Robby.

416
00:15:50,450 --> 00:15:53,660
This is Dr. Santos.
- What? I can't hear you.

417
00:15:53,661 --> 00:15:55,120
- Try the other side.

418
00:15:55,121 --> 00:15:56,746
- I'm Dr. Santos.

419
00:15:56,747 --> 00:15:58,790
Can you hear me?
- Yeah, yeah.

420
00:15:58,791 --> 00:16:01,293
Dante.
Lot of ringing.

421
00:16:01,294 --> 00:16:04,046
- Hey, McKay, Joy,
you're with us.

422
00:16:06,007 --> 00:16:08,592
- One, two, three.

423
00:16:08,593 --> 00:16:12,596
- [groaning]
I need, like, four doctors?

424
00:16:12,597 --> 00:16:14,806
Oh, shit, I knew it was bad.

425
00:16:14,807 --> 00:16:16,600
- This is a teaching hospital.

426
00:16:16,601 --> 00:16:18,810
You get all the extra help
you need.

427
00:16:18,811 --> 00:16:22,063
- Airway and breathing intact.

428
00:16:22,064 --> 00:16:23,815
- Good lung sliding
bilaterally.

429
00:16:23,816 --> 00:16:25,400
- [groaning]

430
00:16:25,401 --> 00:16:26,943
- Tender over the sternum--
possible fracture.

431
00:16:26,944 --> 00:16:28,612
That's your breastbone.
[machine beeps]

432
00:16:28,613 --> 00:16:31,406
- No ectopy on monitor.
- No pericardial effusion.

433
00:16:31,407 --> 00:16:33,783
The heart looks good.
- 130 over 85.

434
00:16:33,784 --> 00:16:36,119
Pulse 108, sats 98 on 2 liters.

435
00:16:36,120 --> 00:16:37,829
- Good enough for 50 of fent.

436
00:16:37,830 --> 00:16:39,789
- He soaked through
his head dressing.

437
00:16:39,790 --> 00:16:41,124
- Let's take a peek.

438
00:16:41,125 --> 00:16:42,459
- Normally, we don't deal
with scalp lacs

439
00:16:42,460 --> 00:16:43,627
on the primary survey,

440
00:16:43,628 --> 00:16:45,295
only if there's
active bleeding.

441
00:16:45,296 --> 00:16:46,630
- With blast injuries,

442
00:16:46,631 --> 00:16:48,465
the pressure wave
can transmit energy

443
00:16:48,466 --> 00:16:50,133
through the body
and cause internal damage

444
00:16:50,134 --> 00:16:52,219
even without
any external visible signs,

445
00:16:52,220 --> 00:16:54,137
especially with organs
that are air filled.

446
00:16:54,138 --> 00:16:56,389
How are we looking, Dr. Santos?
- E-FAST negative.

447
00:16:56,390 --> 00:16:59,643
No free fluid in the belly.
- [groaning]

448
00:16:59,644 --> 00:17:02,312
- Fuck me.
- What--what is it?

449
00:17:02,313 --> 00:17:05,315
- It's a pretty big flap.
Checking for bleeding source.

450
00:17:05,316 --> 00:17:08,193
- Is that through the galea?
- It is, indeed.

451
00:17:08,194 --> 00:17:10,195
- Crack my head open or what?
- Don't worry.

452
00:17:10,196 --> 00:17:12,989
Your skull is intact.
It's just a, uh, very deep cut.

453
00:17:12,990 --> 00:17:15,825
- [groans]
- Ugh.

454
00:17:15,826 --> 00:17:20,121
- Okay, we got a couple
arterioles that need control.

455
00:17:20,122 --> 00:17:22,707
- Figure-of-eight sutures?
- Dr. Santos for 800.

456
00:17:22,708 --> 00:17:24,501
- What are Raney clips, Alex?

457
00:17:24,502 --> 00:17:26,545
Quicker than sutures.
- Ding, ding, ding, ding.

458
00:17:26,546 --> 00:17:28,463
Okay, clamp those bleeders,
call for CT.

459
00:17:28,464 --> 00:17:31,717
I'll be next door, and Duke's
CT line keeps growing.

460
00:17:35,179 --> 00:17:37,180
- 500 cc's out.

461
00:17:37,181 --> 00:17:39,349
- How you doing, Oliver?
- [groans]

462
00:17:39,350 --> 00:17:40,850
- Making some progress in here.

463
00:17:40,851 --> 00:17:42,519
- Can't tell
if it was the phlebotomy

464
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:43,853
or if the nitro
finally kicked in.

465
00:17:43,854 --> 00:17:46,231
- Oh, could have been both.

466
00:17:46,232 --> 00:17:48,066
- Potassium's high. 6.5.

467
00:17:48,067 --> 00:17:49,859
- Be expected
with the missed dialysis

468
00:17:49,860 --> 00:17:51,152
and the added heat stress.

469
00:17:51,153 --> 00:17:53,029
- He needs, uh--
- Dr. Javadi.

470
00:17:53,030 --> 00:17:54,573
- Uh, he needs insulin
and glucose

471
00:17:54,574 --> 00:17:56,032
to shift the potassium
intracellular.

472
00:17:56,033 --> 00:17:58,368
- Exactly.
Uh, 10 units, amp of D-50.

473
00:17:58,369 --> 00:18:00,704
- Start with 5 milligrams
of nebulized albuterol.

474
00:18:00,705 --> 00:18:02,789
It'll shift some potassium
and help his breathing.

475
00:18:02,790 --> 00:18:05,041
- He's at risk for arrhythmias.

476
00:18:05,042 --> 00:18:08,295
- Mm, no peaked T waves,
no QRS widening.

477
00:18:08,296 --> 00:18:10,463
Insulin puts him at risk
for hypoglycemia.

478
00:18:10,464 --> 00:18:12,716
And you tie up nurses
with frequent Accu-Cheks.

479
00:18:12,717 --> 00:18:14,301
- Dr. Al-Hashimi's right.

480
00:18:14,302 --> 00:18:15,885
The nebs will hold him over
until dialysis gets here.

481
00:18:15,886 --> 00:18:17,929
You got this?
- Mm-hmm.

482
00:18:17,930 --> 00:18:20,473
[door opens]

483
00:18:20,474 --> 00:18:22,768
All good in here?
- Yep.

484
00:18:25,187 --> 00:18:27,939
Uh, I'm gonna go give
this guy's kid an update.

485
00:18:27,940 --> 00:18:29,399
He's probably freaking out.

486
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:32,320
Come find me if you need me.
- Uh, yep.

487
00:18:34,614 --> 00:18:36,197
- Um, hey.

488
00:18:36,198 --> 00:18:38,408
Where'd you learn
that shoe trick?

489
00:18:38,409 --> 00:18:41,953
- Oh, I did a clerkship
at NJMS senior care.

490
00:18:41,954 --> 00:18:44,039
One of the attendings
always said you can learn a lot

491
00:18:44,040 --> 00:18:46,082
about your patients
just by looking at their feet.

492
00:18:46,083 --> 00:18:48,043
[bell dings]

493
00:18:48,044 --> 00:18:49,252
- Bravo, ladies.

494
00:18:49,253 --> 00:18:53,048
Now on to the next
lucky customers.

495
00:18:53,049 --> 00:18:54,758
- Probably gonna have
to stay late

496
00:18:54,759 --> 00:18:56,426
to catch up
on all these patients.

497
00:18:56,427 --> 00:18:58,261
- Night-shift reinforcements
will be here soon.

498
00:18:58,262 --> 00:18:59,804
We're supposed to be
speeding up in the 11th hour,

499
00:18:59,805 --> 00:19:01,431
not slowing down.

500
00:19:01,432 --> 00:19:02,807
They don't call it
the final sprint for nothing.

501
00:19:02,808 --> 00:19:04,226
Chop-chop.

502
00:19:08,773 --> 00:19:11,858
- Is that the, uh, mom
of the heatstroke?

503
00:19:11,859 --> 00:19:13,109
- Yeah.

504
00:19:13,110 --> 00:19:16,821
- Do you believe
it was just an accident?

505
00:19:16,822 --> 00:19:18,281
- Does it matter?

506
00:19:18,282 --> 00:19:19,991
- As a doctor, no.

507
00:19:19,992 --> 00:19:22,619
But as a mom...

508
00:19:22,620 --> 00:19:25,497
- Sometimes I can't tell
if motherhood has made me

509
00:19:25,498 --> 00:19:28,459
more understanding
or more judgmental.

510
00:19:30,628 --> 00:19:32,629
- Dana, got a couple visitors.

511
00:19:32,630 --> 00:19:34,798
- Uh, my--my brother.
There was an explosion.

512
00:19:34,799 --> 00:19:36,633
- Hi.
My mom was in a car accident.

513
00:19:36,634 --> 00:19:38,468
- Uh, okay, Monica,
can you help these fine folks

514
00:19:38,469 --> 00:19:40,261
find their loved ones?
- Yeah.

515
00:19:40,262 --> 00:19:42,263
- And, Princess, what are you
still doing here?

516
00:19:42,264 --> 00:19:44,474
Don't you got some crazy luau
pig roast barbecue thing

517
00:19:44,475 --> 00:19:46,434
to get to?
- It's <i>lechón,</i> but, yes, I do.

518
00:19:46,435 --> 00:19:48,812
I'm gonna sneak out in a few.
- Okay, yeah, yeah.

519
00:19:48,813 --> 00:19:50,980
Go have some fun
for both of us, all right?

520
00:19:50,981 --> 00:19:53,942
- Don't stay too late.
- [scoffs] Okay.

521
00:19:53,943 --> 00:19:56,277
- [groans softly]
- Hello, Ms. Stevens.

522
00:19:56,278 --> 00:19:57,487
I'm Dr. McKay.

523
00:19:57,488 --> 00:19:59,572
How you feeling?
- Stupid...

524
00:19:59,573 --> 00:20:02,325
and really, really sore.

525
00:20:02,326 --> 00:20:05,245
- Yeah, we'll, uh, get you
something for your pain, okay?

526
00:20:05,246 --> 00:20:07,038
- [groans]

527
00:20:07,039 --> 00:20:10,041
- Your last patient
left her blanket.

528
00:20:10,042 --> 00:20:11,251
- Oh.

529
00:20:11,252 --> 00:20:13,545
Uh, yeah.

530
00:20:13,546 --> 00:20:15,588
I...

531
00:20:15,589 --> 00:20:17,173
yeah, I'll take it.

532
00:20:17,174 --> 00:20:19,509
Thanks, Esme.

533
00:20:19,510 --> 00:20:21,679
Um, I'll be right back, yeah?

534
00:20:24,890 --> 00:20:27,267
Fuck!
[sighs]

535
00:20:27,268 --> 00:20:29,477
- You good?
- Yeah, yeah.

536
00:20:29,478 --> 00:20:32,856
I'm just--I'm looking
for something to put this in.

537
00:20:32,857 --> 00:20:35,359
A, uh--a patient left it.

538
00:20:37,737 --> 00:20:40,864
She died, the, uh, cancer mom
with the two kids.

539
00:20:40,865 --> 00:20:43,074
- [sighs]
Sounds like a tough one.

540
00:20:43,075 --> 00:20:44,868
- Yeah, that one sucked.

541
00:20:44,869 --> 00:20:47,370
Seeing those two young boys
say goodbye to their mother

542
00:20:47,371 --> 00:20:49,539
was brutal.

543
00:20:49,540 --> 00:20:53,877
Just makes you want to cry.

544
00:20:53,878 --> 00:20:56,045
- You should cry.
You'll feel better.

545
00:20:56,046 --> 00:20:58,381
- I wish I could.

546
00:20:58,382 --> 00:21:02,969
No, I don't even remember
the last time I cried.

547
00:21:02,970 --> 00:21:05,054
I'm not even sure
I can anymore.

548
00:21:05,055 --> 00:21:07,265
Is that what this place
does to you?

549
00:21:07,266 --> 00:21:11,561
- We get pretty good
at shutting down our feelings.

550
00:21:11,562 --> 00:21:13,980
- Yeah, I'm a little scared

551
00:21:13,981 --> 00:21:16,316
I've shut them off for so long,

552
00:21:16,317 --> 00:21:19,903
I don't know how to turn them
back on again.

553
00:21:19,904 --> 00:21:23,491
I just wish I could cry again,
you know?

554
00:21:25,326 --> 00:21:27,744
- You want a hug?

555
00:21:27,745 --> 00:21:29,163
- God, no.

556
00:21:30,748 --> 00:21:32,415
- Your mom is stable,

557
00:21:32,416 --> 00:21:34,918
but she's still waiting
on the imaging of her hip.

558
00:21:34,919 --> 00:21:36,252
We'll know more soon.

559
00:21:36,253 --> 00:21:37,754
You can wait with your dad
in the meantime.

560
00:21:37,755 --> 00:21:38,922
- I've told him a million times

561
00:21:38,923 --> 00:21:40,715
he shouldn't be driving
anymore.

562
00:21:40,716 --> 00:21:43,134
- May I ask what their home
situation is like right now?

563
00:21:43,135 --> 00:21:45,470
- I don't know, they live
over in Squirrel Hill.

564
00:21:45,471 --> 00:21:47,430
Same house I grew up in,
just the two of them.

565
00:21:47,431 --> 00:21:49,390
- We noticed your father

566
00:21:49,391 --> 00:21:51,601
exhibiting signs
of an unsteady gait,

567
00:21:51,602 --> 00:21:54,479
along with other balance
and mobility issues.

568
00:21:54,480 --> 00:21:56,439
- He sees an internist,
neurologist,

569
00:21:56,440 --> 00:21:58,274
cardiologist, gastroenterologist.

570
00:21:58,275 --> 00:22:00,944
He's got a whole team
of "ologists."

571
00:22:00,945 --> 00:22:03,112
Both my parents used to be
in pretty great shape,

572
00:22:03,113 --> 00:22:05,782
but lately, it's like they can
barely get up the stairs.

573
00:22:05,783 --> 00:22:07,659
Half their house
isn't accessible to them.

574
00:22:07,660 --> 00:22:10,161
- Have you looked
into assisted living options?

575
00:22:10,162 --> 00:22:13,665
- Yes, but they refuse
to even talk about it with me.

576
00:22:13,666 --> 00:22:15,959
- Mr. Cohen,
you have a visitor.

577
00:22:15,960 --> 00:22:18,294
- Care Bear.
- Dad.

578
00:22:18,295 --> 00:22:22,340
You feeling okay?
- No, I'm feeling dumb.

579
00:22:22,341 --> 00:22:24,551
- Carrie, this is Dr. King,

580
00:22:24,552 --> 00:22:26,636
who will be assisting
with your mother's care.

581
00:22:26,637 --> 00:22:28,805
I was just explaining
that the treatment

582
00:22:28,806 --> 00:22:30,348
depends on the injuries,

583
00:22:30,349 --> 00:22:32,433
but there are
complicating factors.

584
00:22:32,434 --> 00:22:34,811
We are required
to report impairments

585
00:22:34,812 --> 00:22:36,437
that might affect driving,

586
00:22:36,438 --> 00:22:38,648
and you will likely have
to take another driver's test

587
00:22:38,649 --> 00:22:39,983
to keep your license.

588
00:22:39,984 --> 00:22:41,943
- What's that got
to do with Frida?

589
00:22:41,944 --> 00:22:43,653
- Well, if the hip injury
is serious

590
00:22:43,654 --> 00:22:47,198
and you're also, uh, limited,

591
00:22:47,199 --> 00:22:48,992
then it could mean

592
00:22:48,993 --> 00:22:51,035
an "unsafe to return home"
recommendation for your wife.

593
00:22:51,036 --> 00:22:53,204
- You'd be looking
at a temporary move

594
00:22:53,205 --> 00:22:56,124
to some form of assisted living
and rehabilitation

595
00:22:56,125 --> 00:22:57,208
for Frida's care.

596
00:22:57,209 --> 00:22:59,669
- How temporary?

597
00:22:59,670 --> 00:23:01,796
- Best case,
three to six months,

598
00:23:01,797 --> 00:23:04,674
but she may never return
to the functionality she had

599
00:23:04,675 --> 00:23:06,593
prior to the injury
and recovery.

600
00:23:06,594 --> 00:23:09,846
- Okay, well, kill me now.
- See what I'm dealing with?

601
00:23:09,847 --> 00:23:12,849
Dad, you need to find somewhere
to live that is safer

602
00:23:12,850 --> 00:23:14,559
and healthier for both of you.

603
00:23:14,560 --> 00:23:17,228
- You--you know, the new
communities are not anything

604
00:23:17,229 --> 00:23:18,897
like the old, scary
nursing homes.

605
00:23:18,898 --> 00:23:21,065
They're really nice, and you
get your own apartment.

606
00:23:21,066 --> 00:23:23,192
And, um, you know, my sister
actually lives in one.

607
00:23:23,193 --> 00:23:24,569
She just loves it.

608
00:23:24,570 --> 00:23:26,738
- When the dog dies,
we can talk.

609
00:23:26,739 --> 00:23:28,364
- What if you fall?

610
00:23:28,365 --> 00:23:30,033
What if you have
a heart attack or a stroke,

611
00:23:30,034 --> 00:23:32,035
and there's no one there
to help you?

612
00:23:32,036 --> 00:23:33,953
- You worry too much.
- How can I not?

613
00:23:33,954 --> 00:23:35,997
Look what happened today.

614
00:23:35,998 --> 00:23:38,333
- Okay, we're gonna leave
you two alone.

615
00:23:38,334 --> 00:23:40,711
We'll come back
with Mrs. Cohen.

616
00:23:45,716 --> 00:23:49,218
- Hey, Robby, our scalp victim
is back from CT.

617
00:23:49,219 --> 00:23:51,346
They're prepping him now.
- Shit.

618
00:23:51,347 --> 00:23:53,181
Uh, okay.
I'm coming.

619
00:23:53,182 --> 00:23:55,099
Um...

620
00:23:55,100 --> 00:23:57,685
- Is your friend doing okay?
- He's on my flight risk radar.

621
00:23:57,686 --> 00:24:00,355
- Oh, and Dana put McKay on the
guy who attacked the new nurse.

622
00:24:00,356 --> 00:24:03,775
Is she okay?
- Physically, yes.

623
00:24:03,776 --> 00:24:06,569
I heard you had a little chat
with Langdon earlier.

624
00:24:06,570 --> 00:24:08,154
- Yeah, water under the bridge.

625
00:24:08,155 --> 00:24:09,656
- You have to figure out a way
of working with him

626
00:24:09,657 --> 00:24:11,741
now that he's back.
- Or until he relapses.

627
00:24:11,742 --> 00:24:13,284
- You're becoming
a very good doctor.

628
00:24:13,285 --> 00:24:14,994
Don't let old conflicts
get in the way.

629
00:24:14,995 --> 00:24:16,913
Speaking of which,
I want you and everybody else

630
00:24:16,914 --> 00:24:19,749
to see the trauma counselor
while I'm gone, yes?

631
00:24:19,750 --> 00:24:21,167
- Yes.
- And I asked Whitaker

632
00:24:21,168 --> 00:24:22,794
to house-sit for me
while I'm gone.

633
00:24:22,795 --> 00:24:24,128
I figured that would distract

634
00:24:24,129 --> 00:24:25,588
from the whole
farmer's widow thing.

635
00:24:25,589 --> 00:24:26,673
- Wait, what?
- Bring in the mail,

636
00:24:26,674 --> 00:24:28,424
water the plants--cool?

637
00:24:28,425 --> 00:24:31,094
- Cool. Yeah. Fine.
- That looks good.

638
00:24:31,095 --> 00:24:33,096
You can start the repair
after this one.

639
00:24:33,097 --> 00:24:35,139
- Feeling any pain, sir?

640
00:24:35,140 --> 00:24:38,267
- [groggily]
Uh, no pain, just wet.

641
00:24:38,268 --> 00:24:40,937
- You already numbed him up?
- I did.

642
00:24:40,938 --> 00:24:42,313
- 1% with epi.

643
00:24:42,314 --> 00:24:44,315
- CT back yet?
- No, not yet.

644
00:24:44,316 --> 00:24:46,109
- I watched the slices come up.

645
00:24:46,110 --> 00:24:48,778
Isolated sternal fracture.
Everything else looked normal.

646
00:24:48,779 --> 00:24:51,155
Could send him home,
but given the mechanism,

647
00:24:51,156 --> 00:24:52,949
should probably watch him
overnight.

648
00:24:52,950 --> 00:24:54,826
- I agree with Dr. Garcia.

649
00:24:54,827 --> 00:24:58,579
- I agree with Dr. Garcia, too.

650
00:24:58,580 --> 00:25:02,583
- Three-layer closure--
galea, sub-Q, skin staples.

651
00:25:02,584 --> 00:25:04,002
Enjoy the fireworks.

652
00:25:04,003 --> 00:25:05,962
And hey, if I don't see you
before you leave,

653
00:25:05,963 --> 00:25:07,588
don't forget to buy me
a souvenir,

654
00:25:07,589 --> 00:25:09,632
like a custom elk-bone-carved
hunting blade.

655
00:25:09,633 --> 00:25:11,551
- Nothing less
for my favorite butcher.

656
00:25:11,552 --> 00:25:13,553
- Aww, you're gonna make
a great ex-husband

657
00:25:13,554 --> 00:25:15,764
one day, Robinavitch.

658
00:25:17,558 --> 00:25:19,809
- Hospital's got a defense
attorney looking for Jesse.

659
00:25:19,810 --> 00:25:21,561
Probably took him
to an ICE detention center.

660
00:25:21,562 --> 00:25:23,813
Said we shouldn't hold
our breath, though.

661
00:25:23,814 --> 00:25:25,982
Whatever happened
to "we the people," huh?

662
00:25:25,983 --> 00:25:28,568
<i>[indistinct radio chatter]</i>

663
00:25:28,569 --> 00:25:30,486
Anything back
on that drunk son of a bitch

664
00:25:30,487 --> 00:25:33,322
that attacked Emma?
- Not yet.

665
00:25:33,323 --> 00:25:35,158
- Christ's sake.
- I'm making a run now.

666
00:25:35,159 --> 00:25:36,534
I'll ask.
- Yeah.

667
00:25:36,535 --> 00:25:37,660
- That's the guy
who attacked Emma?

668
00:25:37,661 --> 00:25:40,621
- Mm-hmm.
- That's intense.

669
00:25:40,622 --> 00:25:42,999
How you doing?

670
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:45,126
- Peachy.

671
00:25:45,127 --> 00:25:46,669
- Oh, that's me.

672
00:25:46,670 --> 00:25:48,838
Hey, you did what you had to do
with that guy.

673
00:25:48,839 --> 00:25:51,425
Everything's gonna be fine.
- From your lips.

674
00:25:54,845 --> 00:25:57,180
- Box of class B
launch tubes to the van.

675
00:25:57,181 --> 00:26:00,141
Next second, kaboom!
[chuckles]

676
00:26:00,142 --> 00:26:04,187
I get launched 10, 15 feet
across the garage,

677
00:26:04,188 --> 00:26:07,023
right up into the lift track.

678
00:26:07,024 --> 00:26:09,400
Hit something hard.

679
00:26:09,401 --> 00:26:12,195
Uh, I don't really remember
the rest.

680
00:26:12,196 --> 00:26:15,698
Oh, just my brother screaming,
help, help!

681
00:26:15,699 --> 00:26:19,619
I'm lying there thinking,
oh, he must be dying.

682
00:26:19,620 --> 00:26:21,704
I guess it was me
he was screaming about.

683
00:26:21,705 --> 00:26:23,623
- Your brother is
in the family room.

684
00:26:23,624 --> 00:26:26,626
He's waiting to see you,
and he is alive and well.

685
00:26:26,627 --> 00:26:28,836
[device clicks]
- Oops.

686
00:26:28,837 --> 00:26:30,546
- "Oops"?

687
00:26:30,547 --> 00:26:32,882
What--what's oops mean?

688
00:26:32,883 --> 00:26:36,762
Hello?
- It's nothing to worry about.

689
00:26:38,180 --> 00:26:40,891
We never say "oops"
in front of a patient.

690
00:26:45,229 --> 00:26:47,230
All right, try again.

691
00:26:47,231 --> 00:26:49,273
- You sure?
- Mm-hmm.

692
00:26:49,274 --> 00:26:51,025
- Okay.

693
00:26:51,026 --> 00:26:53,444
Better.
Keep going.

694
00:26:53,445 --> 00:26:54,946
- You might want to leave
the fireworks

695
00:26:54,947 --> 00:26:56,197
to the professionals.

696
00:26:56,198 --> 00:26:58,074
- We are professionals.

697
00:26:58,075 --> 00:27:02,870
Got our official PDA consumer
license a few years back.

698
00:27:02,871 --> 00:27:05,456
Gunpowder's in my blood, man.

699
00:27:05,457 --> 00:27:07,250
Nothing like that...

700
00:27:07,251 --> 00:27:10,711
[whistling, imitates explosion]

701
00:27:10,712 --> 00:27:12,130
- [sighs]
- [chuckles]

702
00:27:12,131 --> 00:27:14,257
Ain't no fighting it.

703
00:27:14,258 --> 00:27:16,092
- Amor fati.

704
00:27:16,093 --> 00:27:18,928
- More like memento mori.

705
00:27:18,929 --> 00:27:21,514
- Tomato, tom-ah-to.

706
00:27:21,515 --> 00:27:23,266
- Am I, like, the only one

707
00:27:23,267 --> 00:27:25,351
who didn't study Latin
for med school?

708
00:27:25,352 --> 00:27:27,103
- Yep.
- Yes.

709
00:27:27,104 --> 00:27:29,105
- And finished.

710
00:27:29,106 --> 00:27:30,773
- Nicely done.

711
00:27:30,774 --> 00:27:32,775
- Can I see my brother now?
- Yep.

712
00:27:32,776 --> 00:27:34,944
- Oh, motherfucker.

713
00:27:34,945 --> 00:27:37,780
- Chest pain?
- Only when I move.

714
00:27:37,781 --> 00:27:39,490
- That would be the fracture
in your sternum,

715
00:27:39,491 --> 00:27:41,284
which we will give you
another 4 of morphine for that.

716
00:27:41,285 --> 00:27:42,660
Thank you.

717
00:27:42,661 --> 00:27:44,579
- And try no laughing
or sneezing

718
00:27:44,580 --> 00:27:46,122
for the next four weeks.

719
00:27:46,123 --> 00:27:47,832
- [chuckles, groans]

720
00:27:47,833 --> 00:27:49,458
- Mr. Haas, we're gonna
switch you over

721
00:27:49,459 --> 00:27:51,961
to a high-flow nasal oxygen
instead of the BiPAP

722
00:27:51,962 --> 00:27:53,754
and see how you do.

723
00:27:53,755 --> 00:27:55,298
- This looks promising.

724
00:27:55,299 --> 00:27:58,134
- Repeat potassium is at 6.1
with the albuterol.

725
00:27:58,135 --> 00:28:01,138
- [breathing deeply]

726
00:28:03,807 --> 00:28:06,309
[sighs]
Feeling a...

727
00:28:06,310 --> 00:28:07,978
a little better.

728
00:28:10,189 --> 00:28:12,940
Don't you ever tell your mother
about how fast you drove here.

729
00:28:12,941 --> 00:28:15,526
- I thought you were gonna die.

730
00:28:15,527 --> 00:28:19,322
- Any longer,
and he might have, so good job.

731
00:28:19,323 --> 00:28:22,909
- Boy just got his permit
last week.

732
00:28:22,910 --> 00:28:24,452
Felt like I was living

733
00:28:24,453 --> 00:28:26,954
in one of those
"Fast and the Furious" movies.

734
00:28:26,955 --> 00:28:30,166
- [chuckles] I can't believe
Pine Ridge closed.

735
00:28:30,167 --> 00:28:31,542
When did that happen?

736
00:28:31,543 --> 00:28:33,836
- I read about it
a few months ago.

737
00:28:33,837 --> 00:28:35,588
Medicare spending cuts.

738
00:28:35,589 --> 00:28:37,757
- Are there any other
emergency rooms in the area?

739
00:28:37,758 --> 00:28:39,091
- Nope.

740
00:28:39,092 --> 00:28:40,676
It's gonna be
a long way to drive

741
00:28:40,677 --> 00:28:42,386
for folks out where we live.

742
00:28:42,387 --> 00:28:44,847
- Mr. Haas, you'll need
to stay here a few hours.

743
00:28:44,848 --> 00:28:46,182
Get you tuned up.

744
00:28:46,183 --> 00:28:49,685
- Yeah. I know the drill.

745
00:28:49,686 --> 00:28:51,812
- Hey, you know, Mason,
if you're hungry,

746
00:28:51,813 --> 00:28:53,522
we've got
a pretty good cafeteria.

747
00:28:53,523 --> 00:28:55,566
- Don't lie to him.

748
00:28:55,567 --> 00:28:57,526
- We have a cafeteria.

749
00:28:57,527 --> 00:28:59,487
- I'm just gonna stay here
with my dad.

750
00:28:59,488 --> 00:29:02,031
- Yeah, sure.

751
00:29:02,032 --> 00:29:04,076
- Thank you for saving him.

752
00:29:10,165 --> 00:29:12,375
- Are you still thinking
rural emergency medicine

753
00:29:12,376 --> 00:29:14,043
when you're done here?

754
00:29:14,044 --> 00:29:17,046
- Um, not thinking, doing.

755
00:29:17,047 --> 00:29:19,048
- God, I hope there are
still some hospitals left

756
00:29:19,049 --> 00:29:20,884
for you to work at.
- Yeah.

757
00:29:22,386 --> 00:29:23,970
- Oh!

758
00:29:23,971 --> 00:29:26,013
So the march of the walking
dead night shift begins.

759
00:29:26,014 --> 00:29:28,057
First one in, Mateo.
You get a gold star.

760
00:29:28,058 --> 00:29:29,475
- What happened in here?

761
00:29:29,476 --> 00:29:31,227
I left you guys
with a layup this morning.

762
00:29:31,228 --> 00:29:32,603
- Don't ask.

763
00:29:32,604 --> 00:29:34,730
- Speaking of layups,
what up, Dr. J?

764
00:29:34,731 --> 00:29:36,482
- Oh, right.

765
00:29:36,483 --> 00:29:39,277
That was, um, some sort of
basketball player, wasn't it?

766
00:29:39,278 --> 00:29:40,820
- Shame on you.

767
00:29:40,821 --> 00:29:43,072
Only the greatest Sixer
to ever play the game.

768
00:29:43,073 --> 00:29:45,366
- Week one of year four
of med school.

769
00:29:45,367 --> 00:29:47,410
You'll be ordering me around
before you know it.

770
00:29:47,411 --> 00:29:49,245
Gonna join the dark side
when you graduate?

771
00:29:49,246 --> 00:29:50,746
- The emergency department?

772
00:29:50,747 --> 00:29:54,208
- The night shift, baby.
It's wild.

773
00:29:54,209 --> 00:29:56,043
- Um...

774
00:29:56,044 --> 00:29:58,713
I haven't even decided what
residency I'm applying for yet.

775
00:29:58,714 --> 00:30:01,132
- You'll figure it out.
Got lots of time.

776
00:30:01,133 --> 00:30:03,759
- Tell that to my parents.

777
00:30:03,760 --> 00:30:06,095
- Dana, results are back
on your guy.

778
00:30:06,096 --> 00:30:07,472
- Give me those.

779
00:30:12,060 --> 00:30:14,061
- I-I choked her?

780
00:30:14,062 --> 00:30:16,439
No, I would never do
something like that.

781
00:30:16,440 --> 00:30:17,606
- But you did.

782
00:30:17,607 --> 00:30:20,943
[slam on window]

783
00:30:20,944 --> 00:30:24,655
Can you, uh--sorry, can you
excuse me for a minute?

784
00:30:24,656 --> 00:30:27,117
Jesus Christ, Dana.
- Read.

785
00:30:29,453 --> 00:30:31,370
What's he saying, anyway?

786
00:30:31,371 --> 00:30:33,581
- He's in a bit of shock.

787
00:30:33,582 --> 00:30:36,625
Claims he doesn't remember
anything.

788
00:30:36,626 --> 00:30:38,961
Says he was on the golf course
getting boozy,

789
00:30:38,962 --> 00:30:40,671
maybe a little too boozy.

790
00:30:40,672 --> 00:30:42,798
Things get hazy, next thing
you know, he's on the floor

791
00:30:42,799 --> 00:30:44,050
and everyone's
screaming at him.

792
00:30:44,051 --> 00:30:46,552
- How convenient.

793
00:30:46,553 --> 00:30:48,471
- Maybe it's better
for both of you

794
00:30:48,472 --> 00:30:50,640
that he doesn't remember
what happened.

795
00:30:52,684 --> 00:30:55,312
- Let me know when you're ready
to take my statement.

796
00:31:01,151 --> 00:31:03,027
- All right, well,
the good news is,

797
00:31:03,028 --> 00:31:05,946
your CT was negative
for skull fracture

798
00:31:05,947 --> 00:31:07,823
or intracranial hemorrhage.

799
00:31:07,824 --> 00:31:11,118
- You mean that's good news for
you and that blonde nurse lady.

800
00:31:11,119 --> 00:31:13,496
- But your lab results
were positive for alcohol

801
00:31:13,497 --> 00:31:15,164
and cocaine.

802
00:31:15,165 --> 00:31:16,957
- [scoffs]

803
00:31:16,958 --> 00:31:18,834
Okay, you got me.

804
00:31:18,835 --> 00:31:20,961
I did a couple
of birdie bumps on the turn,

805
00:31:20,962 --> 00:31:22,880
but we were just having fun
on the golf course.

806
00:31:22,881 --> 00:31:25,091
- Look, when alcohol
is mixed with cocaine,

807
00:31:25,092 --> 00:31:26,842
your liver produces
a metabolite

808
00:31:26,843 --> 00:31:28,260
called cocaethylene.

809
00:31:28,261 --> 00:31:30,262
- What does that
have to do with me

810
00:31:30,263 --> 00:31:33,224
allegedly doing what you said?

811
00:31:33,225 --> 00:31:34,934
- It's a psychoactive molecule

812
00:31:34,935 --> 00:31:37,269
more toxic and potent
than cocaine.

813
00:31:37,270 --> 00:31:41,524
And it can lead to confusion,
paranoia, aggression,

814
00:31:41,525 --> 00:31:44,151
and even psychosis.

815
00:31:44,152 --> 00:31:45,945
- [sighs]

816
00:31:45,946 --> 00:31:48,948
What happens now?

817
00:31:48,949 --> 00:31:51,200
- We're gonna monitor you
for a few hours,

818
00:31:51,201 --> 00:31:53,577
especially given
your likely concussion.

819
00:31:53,578 --> 00:31:54,995
And you'll need to give
a statement

820
00:31:54,996 --> 00:31:57,040
to the police outside
in the meantime.

821
00:32:02,337 --> 00:32:04,505
- Oh, you lucky
this was just a poke.

822
00:32:04,506 --> 00:32:05,673
Spared the muscle.

823
00:32:05,674 --> 00:32:07,258
- See?
It could have been worse.

824
00:32:07,259 --> 00:32:08,884
- No shit, Sherman.

825
00:32:08,885 --> 00:32:10,886
The triangular bayonets
they created back then

826
00:32:10,887 --> 00:32:12,221
were designed to leave wounds

827
00:32:12,222 --> 00:32:14,223
that are nearly impossible
to stitch.

828
00:32:14,224 --> 00:32:16,559
- Those bayonets
had never met me.

829
00:32:16,560 --> 00:32:18,227
- Oh, wow.
- What?

830
00:32:18,228 --> 00:32:20,020
- It's all good.
We got it covered.

831
00:32:20,021 --> 00:32:23,274
- No, are you guys
from the Fort Pitt Museum?

832
00:32:23,275 --> 00:32:25,568
- Yeah. You familiar?
- Yeah.

833
00:32:25,569 --> 00:32:26,986
I love you guys.

834
00:32:26,987 --> 00:32:28,487
My mom would take me
to the fort

835
00:32:28,488 --> 00:32:30,156
every time we'd come down
to visit family as a kid.

836
00:32:30,157 --> 00:32:32,241
- We've been volunteering there
as living historians

837
00:32:32,242 --> 00:32:34,618
for over 20 years.
- I probably saw you then.

838
00:32:34,619 --> 00:32:37,163
Man, I can't wait to tell
my parents about this.

839
00:32:37,164 --> 00:32:38,914
- Getting stabbed
must have been scary.

840
00:32:38,915 --> 00:32:41,417
- Yeah, well, Poindexter here
stumbled over his boots

841
00:32:41,418 --> 00:32:43,085
and nearly impaled me.

842
00:32:43,086 --> 00:32:44,753
- Wouldn't have mattered if you
weren't out of formation.

843
00:32:44,754 --> 00:32:46,088
- You're in good hands.

844
00:32:46,089 --> 00:32:48,007
This guy could have saved
a lot of lives

845
00:32:48,008 --> 00:32:50,801
with his sutures back in 1776.

846
00:32:50,802 --> 00:32:54,096
- I was not out of formation.
- You were out of formation.

847
00:32:54,097 --> 00:32:55,514
- Hi, again.

848
00:32:55,515 --> 00:32:57,224
Good news, Mrs. Cohen.

849
00:32:57,225 --> 00:33:00,060
Your imaging results
show no hip fracture

850
00:33:00,061 --> 00:33:02,104
and no internal bleeding.

851
00:33:02,105 --> 00:33:04,273
But you do have
a small hairline fracture

852
00:33:04,274 --> 00:33:05,941
of the superior pubic ramus.

853
00:33:05,942 --> 00:33:07,943
- Well,
that doesn't sound good.

854
00:33:07,944 --> 00:33:09,111
- Well, if you're gonna
have a fracture,

855
00:33:09,112 --> 00:33:10,404
it's the best one to have.

856
00:33:10,405 --> 00:33:12,823
- Do I need to stay
in the hospital?

857
00:33:12,824 --> 00:33:13,949
- Not necessarily.

858
00:33:13,950 --> 00:33:15,618
Your recovery should be

859
00:33:15,619 --> 00:33:17,620
pretty straightforward
if we can control your pain.

860
00:33:17,621 --> 00:33:20,164
But you need to take it easy
and stay off your feet

861
00:33:20,165 --> 00:33:21,957
for six to eight weeks.

862
00:33:21,958 --> 00:33:23,751
- How can she go home
if she can't walk?

863
00:33:23,752 --> 00:33:26,253
- Oh, she can bear weight
and walk with some help.

864
00:33:26,254 --> 00:33:29,131
And we'll get you a walker
and schedule physical therapy.

865
00:33:29,132 --> 00:33:32,843
- So--so we can go home.
- [laughing] Yes.

866
00:33:32,844 --> 00:33:35,638
- Dad, you can't take care
of Mom all by yourself.

867
00:33:35,639 --> 00:33:37,806
- Oh, we'll manage.

868
00:33:37,807 --> 00:33:40,142
- You guys, you need
to be realistic.

869
00:33:40,143 --> 00:33:43,145
- I don't want strangers
hanging around in the house.

870
00:33:43,146 --> 00:33:45,064
- It's not strangers.
It's helpers.

871
00:33:45,065 --> 00:33:46,815
- Mm-mm, no.
No, thanks.

872
00:33:46,816 --> 00:33:48,985
- Mm-mm.
- Doctors, please.

873
00:33:50,654 --> 00:33:52,655
- I am worried
about your ability

874
00:33:52,656 --> 00:33:55,533
to get the assistance
you need, Mrs. Cohen.

875
00:33:55,534 --> 00:33:57,910
It may be time to think about
some of those

876
00:33:57,911 --> 00:34:01,789
alternative living options
we mentioned.

877
00:34:01,790 --> 00:34:03,499
- I can take care of her.

878
00:34:03,500 --> 00:34:05,793
- Dad, are you gonna be able
to get her into the bathroom

879
00:34:05,794 --> 00:34:07,670
onto the toilet
in the middle of the night?

880
00:34:07,671 --> 00:34:09,672
Are you gonna help her change
her clothes in the morning?

881
00:34:09,673 --> 00:34:11,465
Are you gonna--
- We're--we're fine.

882
00:34:11,466 --> 00:34:15,135
- Dad, please just try it.

883
00:34:15,136 --> 00:34:17,012
You don't have to sell
the house right away.

884
00:34:17,013 --> 00:34:20,224
Just see if you like it.

885
00:34:20,225 --> 00:34:22,685
- You could move in with us.
- No, I can't.

886
00:34:22,686 --> 00:34:24,353
I have work. I have the kids.
I can't.

887
00:34:24,354 --> 00:34:26,313
- No. No. No.

888
00:34:26,314 --> 00:34:28,525
No. Okay?
Just no.

889
00:34:30,527 --> 00:34:33,697
- Carrie, could I speak
with you outside for a minute?

890
00:34:36,866 --> 00:34:38,660
[door opens]

891
00:34:41,371 --> 00:34:43,080
- It's like that every time.

892
00:34:43,081 --> 00:34:44,748
- It's tough to find
the balance

893
00:34:44,749 --> 00:34:46,917
between respecting your
loved ones' needs for agency

894
00:34:46,918 --> 00:34:49,587
and doing what's best
for their safety.

895
00:34:49,588 --> 00:34:53,090
I was wondering if you had
your parents' medication lists

896
00:34:53,091 --> 00:34:56,093
with you or on your phone.
- Yeah.

897
00:34:56,094 --> 00:34:59,430
- I'd like to take a quick
look at it, if that's okay.

898
00:34:59,431 --> 00:35:00,974
- Yeah.

899
00:35:02,934 --> 00:35:05,019
- My man, how you doing?

900
00:35:05,020 --> 00:35:06,270
- Like you better roll in

901
00:35:06,271 --> 00:35:08,230
a bar cart
and a happy hour buffet

902
00:35:08,231 --> 00:35:10,357
if I'm gonna be stuck
in this place much longer.

903
00:35:10,358 --> 00:35:11,734
- This place is a traffic jam.

904
00:35:11,735 --> 00:35:13,110
We're trying to merge you in,
I promise.

905
00:35:13,111 --> 00:35:14,403
- That's no traffic jam.

906
00:35:14,404 --> 00:35:15,904
That's a 20-car pileup.

907
00:35:15,905 --> 00:35:17,948
- The sooner you get out,
the sooner I get out,

908
00:35:17,949 --> 00:35:21,243
so you know
I'm not bullshitting you.

909
00:35:21,244 --> 00:35:23,203
- Why are you jonesing
so hard to get out

910
00:35:23,204 --> 00:35:24,955
and start your ride tonight?

911
00:35:24,956 --> 00:35:26,749
- I've got a schedule.

912
00:35:26,750 --> 00:35:29,628
I've got places to go.
I've got people to see.

913
00:35:33,673 --> 00:35:36,842
I just have to get going.

914
00:35:36,843 --> 00:35:38,927
- You're worried
if you don't leave tonight,

915
00:35:38,928 --> 00:35:41,263
you won't leave at all.

916
00:35:41,264 --> 00:35:43,432
[sighs]
Look, I get it.

917
00:35:43,433 --> 00:35:46,602
I can feel it in the air here.
This place is like quicksand.

918
00:35:46,603 --> 00:35:49,438
- [breathes deeply]
Right.

919
00:35:49,439 --> 00:35:51,190
[clears throat]
- Dr. Robby?

920
00:35:51,191 --> 00:35:55,277
Uh, Dante, the fireworks guy--
something's wrong.

921
00:35:55,278 --> 00:35:56,987
- Okay, shit.
- [sighs]

922
00:35:56,988 --> 00:35:58,822
- Um, do not go anywhere.
You are next up.

923
00:35:58,823 --> 00:36:00,449
I'm gonna deliver you myself.

924
00:36:00,450 --> 00:36:02,368
- I'm starting to feel like
a hostage in this place.

925
00:36:02,369 --> 00:36:04,286
- How do you think I feel?

926
00:36:04,287 --> 00:36:07,332
[indistinct chatter]

927
00:36:10,460 --> 00:36:13,505
[phone ringing]

928
00:36:17,008 --> 00:36:19,176
[monitor beeping rapidly]

929
00:36:19,177 --> 00:36:22,012
What's going on?
- Fucking chest hurts.

930
00:36:22,013 --> 00:36:25,182
[wheezing]
- Systolic's down to 78.

931
00:36:25,183 --> 00:36:27,184
- Blood loss from scalp lac?
- Uh, that's highly unlikely.

932
00:36:27,185 --> 00:36:29,019
Dr. Santos?
- Good breath sounds.

933
00:36:29,020 --> 00:36:31,313
But neck veins are up.
Checking the heart now.

934
00:36:31,314 --> 00:36:33,816
- Okay, 500-cc bolus,
100% non-rebreather please.

935
00:36:33,817 --> 00:36:36,485
- Okay, pericardial effusion,
RV collapse--it's tamponade.

936
00:36:36,486 --> 00:36:38,362
- Yeah, let's prep
for a pericardiocentesis.

937
00:36:38,363 --> 00:36:40,531
- What's going on?
Is he gonna be okay?

938
00:36:40,532 --> 00:36:43,033
You said he was fine.
- CT scan was normal.

939
00:36:43,034 --> 00:36:44,952
- Things change.

940
00:36:44,953 --> 00:36:48,372
- Dante, there is a collection
of blood around your heart.

941
00:36:48,373 --> 00:36:50,833
We need to drain it.
- Oh, fuck.

942
00:36:50,834 --> 00:36:53,001
- Okay, 0.5 per kilo
ketamine, please.

943
00:36:53,002 --> 00:36:54,670
- Won't that drop
his pressure even more?

944
00:36:54,671 --> 00:36:56,004
- We don't have a choice.

945
00:36:56,005 --> 00:36:58,048
We cannot do this
on a moving target.

946
00:36:58,049 --> 00:37:00,509
- Dante, Jesus fucking Christ.
It's my baby brother.

947
00:37:00,510 --> 00:37:02,678
- Hey, Perlah?
- Sir, I'm so sorry,

948
00:37:02,679 --> 00:37:04,221
but you can't
be in here right now.

949
00:37:04,222 --> 00:37:05,973
- What's wrong with him?
Hey, Dante. Dante.

950
00:37:05,974 --> 00:37:08,642
Hey, just hear me--
- Okay. I'm so sorry.

951
00:37:08,643 --> 00:37:10,227
You can't be in there
right now, okay?

952
00:37:10,228 --> 00:37:11,895
We have to stabilize.
- They said he was fine.

953
00:37:11,896 --> 00:37:13,480
- Yeah. Okay.
There's been a complication.

954
00:37:13,481 --> 00:37:15,315
I just need you to take
a couple deep breaths

955
00:37:15,316 --> 00:37:16,984
and try to stay calm.
- Please. Please.

956
00:37:16,985 --> 00:37:19,862
- Okay, just breathe with me.
In. Okay, out.

957
00:37:19,863 --> 00:37:21,697
Look at me.
Breathe in.

958
00:37:21,698 --> 00:37:23,532
- [breathes deeply]
- Out.

959
00:37:23,533 --> 00:37:25,909
Very good.
In, out.

960
00:37:25,910 --> 00:37:28,371
Okay, stay calm, okay?
We got this.

961
00:37:31,207 --> 00:37:32,875
- I can't leave
you <i>pendejos</i> alone.

962
00:37:32,876 --> 00:37:34,543
- Tamponade with shock.
- From what?

963
00:37:34,544 --> 00:37:36,128
- Uh, I'm guessing a small tear

964
00:37:36,129 --> 00:37:37,796
in the right atrium
from the sternal fracture.

965
00:37:37,797 --> 00:37:39,131
- If it was ventricular,

966
00:37:39,132 --> 00:37:40,716
he would have crashed
in the field.

967
00:37:40,717 --> 00:37:42,468
I'll call cardiothoracic.
See if they have an OR.

968
00:37:42,469 --> 00:37:44,011
- Systolic's down to 64.

969
00:37:44,012 --> 00:37:45,387
- Okay, have you ever done
an apical approach?

970
00:37:45,388 --> 00:37:47,765
- Nope.
- No time like the present.

971
00:37:47,766 --> 00:37:49,892
Go lateral to my probe.
- Why not subcostal?

972
00:37:49,893 --> 00:37:52,770
- This is a shorter distance.
There are fewer structures.

973
00:37:52,771 --> 00:37:54,146
If you can see fluid,
there's nothing

974
00:37:54,147 --> 00:37:56,231
between the probe
and the pericardial.

975
00:37:56,232 --> 00:37:58,400
- There? There.
the needle's in the effusion.

976
00:37:58,401 --> 00:38:01,779
- Mm-hmm. Draw back.
- Good blood return.

977
00:38:01,780 --> 00:38:03,655
- Okay, sometimes it only takes
20 cc's

978
00:38:03,656 --> 00:38:05,407
to let that heart fill again.

979
00:38:05,408 --> 00:38:08,243
- Carotid pulse much stronger.
- Thanks. Be right up.

980
00:38:08,244 --> 00:38:09,912
They'll have a room
ready in five.

981
00:38:09,913 --> 00:38:12,414
- All right,
that is 40 cc's out.

982
00:38:12,415 --> 00:38:14,041
- Blood pressure's up.
90 systolic.

983
00:38:14,042 --> 00:38:15,793
- Okay, now we're gonna place
the J wire,

984
